From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:14:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] notmuch: Tag patches reported committed
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: louvprnwkoxmwzywloqvvnqksswtqvxy

Patches are applied from the list without a reply from the maintainer,
so the review queue cannot drop them by tag, and committed patches
stay in it until someone checks trunk by hand.

A message that reports a patch committed names it in a Committed
header, as just committed on the mail branch writes.  Deriving the
tag from that header, like replied from In-Reply-To, keeps it a pure
function of the list: it needs no parsing of prose, comes back the
same when the database is rebuilt, and disappears if the report is
dropped.  The review queue can then exclude tag:committed.
---
This reads the header that "[PATCH] mail: Add just committed to report
patches trunk took" writes on the mail branch, posted next.  The two
apply to different branches and either is harmless without the other.

The In-Reply-To parser becomes header_ids, which takes the header's
name, so Committed reuses it rather than copying it.

Tested against a scratch copy of the list with its own notmuch
database: the four patches trunk took unchanged were tagged
committed, the replied and inbox tags came out identical to the
current script's, and removing one report removed its tag on the next
run.

